<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Rani</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 1168 CE (approx.) · capital Arkona</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Slavic people on the island of Rügen whose temple of Svantevit at Arkona was the last great pagan sanctuary on the Baltic until the Danes destroyed it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Rani were West Slavic settlers of the island of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-rugen">Rügen</a> and nearby coast from around the 8th or 9th century. Their island position and the prestige of their sanctuary gave them influence over neighbouring Slavic peoples, and they are mentioned in German and Danish chronicles from the 10th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Rani were ruled by princes, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxo-grammaticus">Saxo Grammaticus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helmold-of-bosau">Helmold of Bosau</a> describe the high priest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkona">Arkona</a> as the real power. The temple of the four-faced god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svantevit">Svantevit</a> stood on a cliff at Cape Arkona, receiving tribute from other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> and keeping a sacred white horse for divination. The Rani raided Danish coasts and traded across the Baltic.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1168 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-i-of-denmark">King Valdemar I of Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absalon">Bishop Absalon</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkona">Arkona</a> and destroyed the idol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svantevit">Svantevit</a>. Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaromar-i-of-rugen">Jaromar I</a> accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and Danish overlordship, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-rugen">Principality of Rügen</a>, which lasted until its absorption into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania</a> in 1325.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 800 CE – 1168 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:75.62%;width:7.32%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 CE – 1168 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Arkona</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tetzlav, Jaromar I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Slavic paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polabian (West Slavic)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rujani, Rugians of Rügen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=984&amp;focus=rani" aria-label="Rani on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4917 309 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Rani lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5237 565L5274 535M5237 565L5296 569"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5274 535h0M5296 569h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5237 565h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5237 565h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Arkona. <a href="/atlas?year=984&amp;focus=rani">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
